  "mechanismSummary": "Acid-catalysed enolisation turns acetonedicarboxylic acid into its enol: H₃O⁺ protonates the ketone and water removes a doubly activated α-proton. The enol attacks the cyclic iminium ion (an intermolecular Mannich reaction), and water removes the proton from the protonated ketone. The product keeps a hemiaminal OH on the ring and a second enolisable CH₂ on the chain. The two CO₂H groups are drawn un-ionised for clarity; near pH 7 they are mostly carboxylates, and Robinson used the calcium salt of the acid.",
